<revisionlist>
  <revision rev="1" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>515ad661cfdf83d47ec2438d3366cdbb</srcmd5>
    <version>2.60.1</version>
    <time>1787323937</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>New package: picsart-gen-ai 2.60.1, Picsart's CLI for their generative AI
service.

This replaces SR 1370570, which submitted the same sources under the name
gen-ai and has been revoked. Reviewer bigironman asked there: &quot;Since this
is specific to one service, is the package name really a good idea ?&quot; That
is a fair objection - the tool is a client for one specific hosted service
and should not hold a generic name - so the package is renamed to
picsart-gen-ai. devel:tools/gen-ai has been deleted.

The installed command deliberately stays /usr/bin/gen-ai. That is upstream's
own CLI name, the one all upstream documentation and every npx invocation
use, so renaming it would diverge from upstream for no gain; the RPM name is
what carries the service qualification. This follows the usual practice of
not renaming upstream commands (git-core ships /usr/bin/git, python3-pip
ships /usr/bin/pip). The package ships no man page, shell completion or
desktop file, so /usr/bin/gen-ai and the module directory are its only
claims on the name, and no other package in OBS ships a binary called
gen-ai. A spec comment above the symlink records the choice so it can be
pushed back on.

The upstream tarball, the @picsart/gen-ai directory under
/usr/lib/node_modules and the command all keep the upstream name through an
npm_name macro, so the built payload is identical to what 1370570 carried
apart from the docdir and licensedir paths.

There is an Obsoletes on gen-ai for anyone who installed it from
devel:tools, but deliberately no matching Provides: providing the generic
name back would undo the point of the rename, and nothing requires it. That
is why rpmlint reports obsolete-not-provided - it is intended, and the spec
says so. The bound is version-only (gen-ai &lt;= %{version}) because both
packages build at the same release in the devel project, so a
release-qualified &quot;&lt;&quot; would compare equal and never fire.

Generates and edits images, video and audio from the terminal against
Picsart's hosted API (a Picsart account is required; nothing runs locally).
MIT, noarch, pure JavaScript on nodejs &gt;= 22.

Packaged from the published npm bundle: the upstream git tree inlines two
dependencies from a private registry (@pulse/core, @pulse/server) that are
not publishable to npm, so only the published artifact is buildable
downstream. Runtime dependencies are vendored with the node_modules OBS
service (package-lock.json + node_modules.obscpio consumed offline by
local-npm-registry at build time), one Source URL per dependency in
node_modules.spec.inc. The License tag covers the vendored tree; the
derivation is recorded in a Legal-Review-Notice comment above it.

Built green natively on aarch64, rpmlint 0 errors / 0 badness, the check
section runs the packaged CLI.</comment>
    <requestid>1371038</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="2" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>0747dbd28028da8c3c999710a0e5b590</srcmd5>
    <version>2.68.1</version>
    <time>1787566196</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>- Update to version 2.68.1:
  * Update the inlined @picsart/ai-sdk to 5.x so edit, enhance,
    upscale, TTS and related flows match the new catalog (they
    selected no models after the SDK 5 shape change) and hide
    deprecated models
  * Fix a crash after generation when xdg-open is missing, Ctrl+C
    cancel handling, prompt-box corruption, wide-character widths
    and ANSI sequences leaking into piped stdout
  * Harden login and token refresh (timeouts, atomic writes, CI
    PICSART_ACCESS_TOKEN, 401 retry) and reject directory uploads
  * Sanitize untrusted API and model names so they cannot inject
    terminal escape sequences
  * Wire video-extend array file slots, keep edit-mode parameters
    and expand ~ in downloadDir
  * Honour config defaultModel and imagePreview</comment>
    <requestid>1373009</requestid>
